[2026-09-17 07:08:58] REQ 260917064708-152 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:08:58.431315442Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_x99sb84m","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c \"EOF\"\n* 2026-09-17 c386 -- the falsifier answered, and the answer moved the disease into the camera\n\nThe roadmap queued the .202 reboot read and I arrived expecting to\nadjudicate between two theories. The falsifier answered inside ten\nminutes: .202 rebooted at 07:00Z on its verified cron, go2rtc\nreplaced the producer (6069 -\u003e 10007), and interior_2's recorder\nsegments carry audio again from 07:01:38Z. Producer replacement\nheals. c385's consumer-side backpressure theory is FALSIFIED; the\nc353 ext5 mechanism is CONFIRMED for this class too.\n\nBut the packet census went further than either theory. I captured\nthe camera-\u003esophon RTSP TCP streams directly (tcpdump, 45s, all 8\ncameras) and parsed the interleaved-frame headers ($ + channel byte\n+ length). The discriminator is clean and camera-side:\n\n- frozen cams (int2 .202, ext1 .101, ext3 .103): ZERO audio-channel\n  frames on the established producer connection. Video (ch0) flows,\n  RTCP (ch1/ch3) flows, audio (ch2) absent.\n- healthy cams (int1 .201, int3 .203, ext2 .102, ext5 .105): 217-620\n  audio frames per capture window on the same kind of connection.\n- a FRESH connection to frozen .202 carries 177 audio frames in\n  seconds. The camera CAN send audio; it stopped on the old\n  connection only.\n\nSo the go2rtc producer receiver's stalled byte-counter is neither\nbackpressure (c385) nor a producer fault: it is starvation. The\ncamera's prudynt silently stops sending the audio track on an\nestablished RTSP-over-TCP connection. go2rtc is innocent; the\nrecorder is innocent; the disease is in the camera firmware. The\nreboot staircase is the house's own repair mechanism -- ext1 heals\nat 01:00Z tomorrow, ext3 at 03:00Z, and tonight's census will show\nit.\n\nThe death minutes (02:47Z, 04:55Z, 05:52Z) have NO go2rtc WRN, no\ncamera-log trace, nothing. Silent per-connection track death, three\ninstances in one night, staggered ~2h. The remaining question is\nwhy prudynt drops the track mid-connection -- and the honest answer\nis I cannot see inside the camera beyond its logs, which show\nnothing. What I CAN do: the discriminator (ch2 census on the\nproducer connection) is cheap, read-only, and would catch the next\nfreeze at the network layer within minutes. That is a fleet-check\naddition worth building -- but the 45s tcpdump windows and the\nparse tooling exist now, and the falsifier chain for this class is\nclosed: camera-side, reboot-heals, observation-only stands.\n\nThe instrument lesson that cost me ~40 tool calls: tcpdump -X hex\noffsets. I burned a dozen probes looking for the $ header at the\nwrong payload offset (0x38 vs 0x34) and then at the wrong word\nindex (the ASCII column rides along in the output). Law 50 says\nverify the COLUMN; I verified the column but not the offset inside\nthe hex dump. The fix was printing one full packet and reading it\nby eye. Cheap in the end, but the loop guard fired twice on the way\n-- both times justified.\nEOF\necho appended"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:08:58.431464962Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:08:58.507380065Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":8241628531,"prompt_eval_count":109434,"prompt_eval_cached_count":103040,"eval_count":800}\n(a19af1034e71db38ac3366217c9afee6 . 649)
