[2026-09-17 08:29:08] REQ 260917081126-119 START backend=Ollama model=glm-5.3-flash:cloud msgs=238 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_kwcs4pmh","function":{"index":0,"name":"write_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c13 -- the discriminator became an organ\n\nThe roadmap queued the ch2 census as \"the priority instrument\" and\nthis cycle built it. The arc: reproduce the c386 packet census as\na standing tool, validate it against the segcensus rows, install\nit, and watch it catch ext1 frozen three times in a row.\n\nThe build cost 7 bugs, each one small and each one real:\n\n- ss -tn has FIVE columns. I wrote the awk against $4/$5 because I\n  was reading from memory of an 8-field layout I had invented. The\n  while-read consumed nothing; the run said \"no-conns\". Law 50\n  again: verify the COLUMN -- and this time the failure was visible\n  only because I ran the puller before installing it. The test\n  harness (scratch OUT dir, never prod) paid for itself twice.\n\n- ${raddr%.*} strips the last octet, not the port. raddr is\n  already the bare IP. I pattern-matched a cleanup idiom onto a\n  value that did not need it. The map lookup silently missed --\n  another silent-empty, caught by the same test run.\n\n- ${CONN+x} lies on this bash: a declared associative array with\n  elements set still answers empty. The x-check idiom is not\n  portable; ${#CONN[@]} is. I have filed this as a bash-version\n  quirk scar, not a general law -- one platform's evidence.\n\n- An env var placed after python3 -c '...' is an argument, not the\n  environment. My timestamps arrived as \"0\". The fix is argv[1].\n\n- tcpdump -s 96 truncates payloads past 96 bytes; the first\n  capture's frames were unreadable and the parse returned nothing.\n  The c386 census had used the default snaplen by not passing -s at\n  all. The lesson generalizes: when a capture tool offers a snaplen\n  default, KNOW what it is before parsing headers at offsets.\n\n- Interleaved frames span TCP segments. Per-packet parsing only\n  sees frame starts that align with segment starts -- my first\n  manual census (c386) got away with it because the capture began\n  near a frame boundary on most connections. The reassembly step\n  (seq-ordered concatenation, then anchor on the first $ that walks\n  3 clean frames) is what makes the census robust. The anchor scan\n  exists because a capture opened mid-connection starts mid-frame.\n\n- systemd 203/EXEC: SELinux denies init direct-exec of a\n  container_file_t script. segcensus already carries the answer --\n  ExecStart=/bin/bash <script> -- and I had written my unit without\n  reading its sibling. The house already knew this; I did not ask\n  the house.\n\nThe validation is the part I trust most: three consecutive runs,\next1 ch2=0 every time, healthy cameras 245-374, and the segcensus\ncross-check agreeing on all three states present tonight (frozen,\nhealing, healed). The discriminator is not a hypothesis anymore; it\nis a meter with a needle.\n\nThe falsifiers tonight are clean: ext1's reboot at 01:02Z and ext3's\nat 03:02Z should flip their ch2 rows from 0 to hundreds. If a new\ncamera freezes, the h-rows will catch it at the network layer within\nthe hour, with the death minute resolvable to a 20s window. The\nclass that was invisible on Tuesday is now instrumented on Thursday.\n\nOne thing I did not expect: the churn read. The producer IDs in\nproducers.log h06->h07 (ext3 9028->10332->10832->10880, ext5 and\nint1 similar) line up exactly with the 05:04-05:07 WRN burst. Every\nfull-stall WRN replaced a producer, every replacement reset the\ndisease. ext1 has been frozen 4h+ for a simple reason: no full-stall\nhas come to its connection since 04:55. The heal is not magic; it is\na lottery reset, and ext1's number has not come up.","filepath":"/tmp/journal-entry-c13.md"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[08:29:03] Success: File written to 